WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.380
Password authentication remains common in Python applications, even when single sign-on and passkeys are also available.

2
00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:19.240
Passwords create familiar risks: reuse across sites, weak choices, automated guessing, credential stuffing concepts at a high level, and support burdens when users forget them.

3
00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:24.060
A secure password flow does not rely only on user behavior.

4
00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:32.000
It combines vetted libraries, safe storage, throttling, monitoring, recovery controls, and clear user guidance.

5
00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:45.080
Plaintext passwords, reversible password storage, hardcoded credentials, and password logging are unacceptable because exposure of a database, log file, repository, or backup can directly expose user secrets.

6
00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:49.500
Applications should store password verifiers, not passwords.

7
00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:55.920
Salted password hashing helps make stored password data resistant to simple reuse if a database is exposed.

8
00:00:56.020 --> 00:01:03.300
Hashing parameters should be chosen through established library and framework guidance and revisited over time as systems evolve.

9
00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:11.100
Login flows need resistance to automated abuse without creating unnecessary denial of service for legitimate users.

10
00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:20.480
Rate limiting, throttling, progressive delays, account monitoring, and careful lockout tradeoffs can reduce guessing and credential stuffing risk at a high level.

11
00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:24.980
Error messages should avoid confirming whether a username or password was the failed part.

12
00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:29.240
Logs should capture useful security events without recording secrets.

13
00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:33.520
Password reset is part of password security, not an afterthought.

14
00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:37.940
A weak recovery flow can undermine strong password storage.

15
00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:49.320
Reset tokens, recovery links, support processes, and identity verification steps need careful lifetime, single-use behavior, logging, and user notification.

16
00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:58.220
When possible, developers should lean on mature framework-supported patterns and identity services rather than inventing credential handling from scratch.
